Java 21 Features — Interview Focus ⭐⭐⭐

Java 21 is an LTS release and is especially important for modern Spring Boot + MicroservicesDesignPattern interviews.

The biggest Java 21 feature is Virtual Threads.

1. Virtual Threads ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Virtual threads are lightweight threads designed for high-throughput concurrent applications.

Traditional Platform Thread
Thread thread = new Thread(() -> {
    System.out.println("Hello");
});

thread.start();

Platform threads are relatively expensive because they are backed by OS threads.

Java 21 Virtual Thread
Thread.startVirtualThread(() -> {
    System.out.println("Hello from virtual thread");
});

Or:

Thread thread = Thread.ofVirtual()
        .start(() -> {
            System.out.println("Hello");
        });
ExecutorService
try (ExecutorService executor =
         Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {

    executor.submit(() -> {
        System.out.println("Task running");
    });
}
Why Virtual Threads?

Suppose your application receives:

10,000 requests

and many requests spend time waiting for:

Database
REST API
Kafka
File I/O
Network

Virtual threads allow you to handle many concurrent tasks without needing one expensive OS thread per task.

Interview answer

Virtual threads are lightweight threads managed by the JVM rather than directly mapping one-to-one with OS threads. They are particularly useful for high-concurrency, I/O-bound workloads.

⚠️ Important

Virtual threads are not automatically faster for CPU-intensive work.

Good:

REST call
    ↓
Database call
    ↓
Wait
    ↓
Response

Less useful:

Heavy CPU calculation
    ↓
Heavy CPU calculation
2. Record Patterns ⭐⭐⭐

Java 21 allows you to destructure records directly.

Suppose:

record Employee(String name, int age) {}

Instead of:

Employee e = new Employee("Ashutosh", 36);

System.out.println(e.name());
System.out.println(e.age());

You can use a record pattern:

if (obj instanceof Employee(String name, int age)) {
    System.out.println(name);
    System.out.println(age);
}
Easy trick

Record Pattern = extract record values directly

3. Pattern Matching for switch ⭐⭐⭐

Java 21 made pattern matching for switch a standard feature.

static String test(Object obj) {

    return switch (obj) {

        case Integer i -> "Integer: " + i;

        case String s -> "String: " + s;

        case Double d -> "Double: " + d;

        default -> "Unknown";
    };
}

Now switch can work with types, not just constants.

Before
if (obj instanceof String) {
    ...
} else if (obj instanceof Integer) {
    ...
}
Java 21
switch (obj) {
    case String s -> ...
    case Integer i -> ...
    default -> ...
}
4. Sequenced Collections ⭐⭐

Java 21 introduced interfaces for collections where encounter order matters.

Important interfaces:

SequencedCollection
SequencedSet
SequencedMap

For example:

SequencedCollection<String> list =
        new ArrayList<>();

list.add("A");
list.add("B");
list.add("C");

System.out.println(list.getFirst());
System.out.println(list.getLast());

Output:

A
C

You can also reverse the view:

list.reversed();
5. String Templates ⚠️

Java 21 introduced String Templates as a preview feature, not a permanent standard feature.

Example from the preview:

String name = "Ashutosh";
String result = STR."Hello \{name}";

However, String Templates were later withdrawn, so don't describe them as a normal Java 21 production feature in an interview.

6. Unnamed Patterns and Variables

Java 21 introduced _ for certain unused variables/patterns as a preview feature.

Example:

if (obj instanceof String _) {
    System.out.println("It is a String");
}

The idea is:

I know the type, but I don't need the variable.

7. Unnamed Classes and Instance Main Methods ⚠️

Java 21 introduced this as a preview feature to make simple Java programs easier to write.

Traditional:

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }
}

The preview feature allows simpler beginner-friendly forms.

Don't prioritize this for normal enterprise interviews.

🔥 Java 21 vs Java 17
Java 17	Java 21
Records	Record Patterns
Sealed Classes	Pattern Matching for switch
Pattern Matching instanceof	Virtual Threads ⭐
Text Blocks	Sequenced Collections
Switch Expressions	Unnamed patterns/variables
	Unnamed classes/main methods
⭐ Most Important for Your Interviews

For Java + Spring Boot + MicroservicesDesignPattern, learn Java 21 in this order:

1️⃣ Virtual Threads ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()
2️⃣ Pattern Matching for switch ⭐⭐⭐⭐
case String s -> ...
case Integer i -> ...
3️⃣ Record Patterns ⭐⭐⭐⭐
if (obj instanceof Employee(String name, int age))
4️⃣ Sequenced Collections ⭐⭐⭐
getFirst()
getLast()
reversed()
🧠 Easy Java Version Trick

Remember the major versions like this:

Java 8
 ↓
Lambda + Stream + Optional

Java 11
 ↓
String + HttpClient + Files

Java 17
 ↓
Record + Sealed + Pattern Matching

Java 21
 ↓
Virtual Threads + Pattern Switch + Record Pattern
🎯 Interview one-liner

If interviewer asks:

"What are the major features introduced in Java 21?"

Say:

"Java 21 is an LTS release. Its major features include Virtual Threads, Pattern Matching for switch, Record Patterns, and Sequenced Collections. Among these, Virtual Threads are particularly important for high-concurrency I/O-bound applications such as REST APIs and microservices."